# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
sp("V04-A-001", "Philosophy and ethics", "Forgiving is nearly the opposite of excusing, so excusing everything leaves nothing to forgive.", "Easy",
   ["Forgiveness is often confused with excusing, but the two are closer to opposites than to near relations.",
    "To excuse someone is to decide that what they did was not really their fault: they were tired, misled or under pressure, and so there is nothing to hold against them.",
    "This is why forgiveness can be offered only by someone who has been wronged, and why it can cost her something: the resentment she surrenders was hers by right.",
    "An excuse costs nothing of the kind, since it removes the very ground of complaint.",
    "It follows that the more readily we excuse, the less there is left for us to forgive.",
    "A person who explains away every injury may seem generous, but he has quietly declined to treat others as responsible for what they do."],
   "To forgive, by contrast, is to accept that the wrong was real and was theirs, and then to give up the resentment one is entitled to feel.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence defines forgiving 'by contrast' with excusing, so it must follow the account of excusing. It must also come before 'This is why forgiveness can be offered only by someone who has been wronged', since that claim, and the 'resentment she surrenders', follow from forgiveness accepting that 'the wrong was real' and giving up resentment. Only gap (2) lies between the two.",
   {1: "At gap (1) excusing has not yet been explained, so 'by contrast' has nothing to contrast with, and the sentence about excusing would then be followed by a claim about forgiveness that does not follow from it.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already drawn the consequences of forgiving, including the surrendered resentment, so supplying the definition now comes after its use and merely repeats what has been said.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached its conclusion about the person who excuses everything; defining forgiveness at the end leaves the earlier 'This is why' without its basis."},
   "Placing the definition at gap (3), next to the remark that an excuse costs nothing, because both sentences compare excusing with forgiving.",
   "V04A-forgiving-and-excusing", "A contrast marker and the following 'This is why' fix one position; the other gaps are clearly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
sp("V04-A-002", "Psychology", "Plans run late because the inside view leaves out the interruptions that befall nearly every project.", "Medium",
   ["People who plan a project routinely underestimate how long it will take, even when they have finished similar projects late many times before.",
    "What makes the error puzzling is not its existence but its persistence, since experience ought to correct it.",
    "The usual explanation is that planners take an inside view: they picture the particular steps of the task in front of them and add up the time each step seems to need.",
    "Such a picture is built out of intentions, and intentions contain no interruptions.",
    "The proposed remedy is to take an outside view, setting aside the details of the project and asking how long projects of the same general kind have actually taken.",
    "This feels like throwing information away, because it ignores everything the planner knows about her own case.",
    "In fact it restores the information that matters most, the record of how such projects usually go wrong, which the inside view had quietly left out."],
   "Real projects, by contrast, are seldom spared them: an illness, a late delivery or a resignation befalls almost every one, though no single one could have been foreseen.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The pronoun 'them' in the missing sentence needs plural interruptions to refer to, and 'by contrast' sets real projects against something that lacks interruptions. Both point to the sentence ending 'intentions contain no interruptions'. The sentence must also come before the remedy, which answers exactly this gap between plan and reality, so it belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) neither the inside view nor interruptions have been mentioned, so 'them' has no referent and 'by contrast' has nothing to contrast with.",
    2: "At gap (2) the inside view has not yet been described and interruptions have not been mentioned, so 'them' has nothing to refer to and 'by contrast' sets real projects against nothing.",
    5: "At gap (4) the remedy has already been proposed; the sentence would cut 'This feels like throwing information away' off from the remedy that 'This' refers to, and 'them' would reach back past the remedy to interruptions two sentences earlier."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (4), as if real projects were being contrasted with the outside view rather than with intentions.",
   "V04A-planning-and-delay", "The solver must track the plural 'them' to the word 'interruptions' and see that the sentence must precede the remedy it motivates.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 003
sp("V04-A-003", "Society and culture", "Renamed streets keep their old names in speech because a name is shared local knowledge that an order cannot erase.", "Easy",
   ["When a city renames a street, the new name appears overnight on signs and maps, but it often takes a generation to appear in everyday speech.",
    "That knowledge is spread across thousands of small habits: residents give directions by the old name, shopkeepers print it on their cards, and older people use it to locate their memories.",
    "An official order can change the label, but it cannot reach what people already know, and so the old name lingers in conversation long after it has vanished from the signs.",
    "Newcomers, who never knew the old name, adopt the new one almost at once.",
    "For a while the city speaks in two vocabularies, and the change is complete only when those who learned the old name are no longer there to use it."],
   "The reason is that a street name is not only an official label but also a piece of shared local knowledge.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence explains the delay described in the opening sentence and introduces 'shared local knowledge'. The next sentence begins 'That knowledge', which needs exactly this antecedent. The sentence therefore belongs at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) 'That knowledge' in the previous sentence would have had nothing to refer to, and the reason for the delay would arrive after its illustrations.",
    3: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already explained why the old name lingers; giving 'The reason' again would repeat a point already made and separate the old residents from the newcomers they are contrasted with.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has described the end of the change; a reason for the original delay comes far too late."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2) because it seems to explain the habits just listed.",
   "V04A-street-renaming", "A forward set-up locked by 'That knowledge'; the wrong gaps are easy to reject.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

