sp("V04-A-004", "History and politics", "The early optical telegraph shows that access, not speed, was the decisive question in communication.", "Medium",
   ["Long before electricity carried messages, France in the 1790s began building chains of hilltop towers whose movable arms could be set in many distinct positions, each standing for a letter, word or phrase.",
    "An operator watched the neighbouring tower through a telescope, copied its signal and passed it on, so that a message could cross the country far faster than any rider.",
    "The system was impressive, but its limits were as instructive as its speed.",
    "It worked only in daylight and in clear weather, and every tower needed trained staff who did nothing but watch and repeat.",
    "Above all, it was built to serve the state, which saw in rapid communication a means of holding a large territory together.",
    "Private citizens were generally not permitted to send messages on it, and the network was never meant to become a public service."],
   "The speed of a message, in other words, mattered less to the history of communication than the question of who was permitted to send one.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence restates, 'in other words', a point about who was 'permitted to send' messages. That point is made only by the final sentence, which says private citizens were 'not permitted to send messages on it'. The sentence is therefore the paragraph's conclusion and belongs at gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) only the working of the towers has been described; nothing about who could use them has been said, so 'in other words' has nothing to restate.",
    3: "At gap (2) the paragraph has just promised to discuss the system's limits; a conclusion about permission would come before any of those limits is described.",
    5: "At gap (3) the state's purpose has been named, but the exclusion of private citizens has not; the sentence would restate a point not yet made and leave the next sentence as a detail tacked on after the conclusion."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), right after the state is named, because state control seems to imply who may send.",
   "V04A-optical-telegraph", "The tempting gap (3) mentions the state; only careful reading of 'permitted to send' shows the conclusion needs the final sentence's 'not permitted to send messages'.")

