sp("V04-A-006", "Science and environment", "The openness that lets lichens live anywhere makes them sensitive recorders of air quality.", "Medium",
   ["A lichen looks like a single plant, a crust or a leafy fringe on a rock or a branch, but it is in fact a partnership.",
    "A fungus provides the structure and holds water, while an alga or a bacterium living inside it makes food from sunlight.",
    "Together they can live where neither could survive alone: on bare stone, on desert soil, on the bark of trees in the coldest forests.",
    "Because a lichen has no roots and no protective outer skin, it takes in water and nutrients directly from rain and from the air around it.",
    "Many species disappear from city centres where sulphur dioxide is common, and some return when the air is cleaned.",
    "For this reason, surveys of lichens have long been used as a cheap way of reading the quality of the air in a district.",
    "A lichen does not so much measure pollution as record its history, since it grows slowly and responds to years of exposure rather than to a single bad day."],
   "Yet the very openness that lets it live on bare rock leaves it with no defence against whatever else the air contains.",
   [2, 3, 4, 7],
   "'The very openness' refers back to the description of a lichen taking in water and nutrients directly from rain and air, and 'Yet' turns that strength into a weakness. The weakness must be stated before the evidence that species vanish from polluted city centres. Only gap (3) lies between the two sentences.",
   {2: "At gap (1) the lichen's hardiness has not even been described yet, so 'Yet' has no strength to turn against, and the 'openness' has not been introduced.",
    3: "At gap (2) the hardiness has been described, but the openness, the absence of roots and skin, comes only in the next sentence, so 'the very openness' has no referent.",
    7: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already used the vulnerability to explain surveys of air quality; stating it only at the end leaves the vanishing of species from cities unexplained when it is reported."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), right after the list of harsh places, because it seems to qualify the lichen's hardiness.",
   "V04A-lichens-air", "Two adjacent gaps both follow talk of hardiness; the solver must see that 'openness' is introduced only by the rootless-absorption sentence.")

