sp("V04-A-009", "Society and culture", "The move from letters to digital messages may leave future historians with fewer intimate records.", "Easy",
   ["Historians of the last few centuries have relied heavily on private letters.",
    "A letter was written for one reader, often in haste, and it preserved opinions and feelings that its writer would never have put into print.",
    "Families kept such letters in drawers and attics, and a great many of them eventually found their way into libraries and archives.",
    "Messages of this kind are easy to delete, and the accounts that hold them are often closed when their owners die.",
    "No one clears out an attic full of messages after a funeral, because there is no attic to clear.",
    "Future historians may therefore find that the age which wrote more than any before it has left fewer intimate records behind."],
   "Today, much of that private correspondence has moved to email and messaging services, where it is stored on servers that its writers do not control.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence moves from the past to the present and introduces email and messaging services. The next sentence must then be 'Messages of this kind are easy to delete', which refers back to those services. The sentence therefore follows the account of letters reaching archives and fills gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the nature of letters has not yet been described, and the following sentence ('A letter was written') would jump back to the past straight after the shift to the present.",
    2: "At gap (2) the sentence would interrupt the history of letters, and 'such letters' in the next sentence would then seem to refer to emails kept in attics.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already discussed deleted messages and missing attics; the emails must be introduced before 'Messages of this kind' can refer to them."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2) because it continues the discussion of correspondence.",
   "V04A-letters-and-archives", "A plain backward reference ('Messages of this kind') fixes the position.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

