sp("V04-A-010", "History and politics", "Late medieval quarantine was not mere luck: it acted on an observed pattern without a correct theory.", "Medium",
   ["The practice of holding ships and travellers in isolation for a fixed period before they could enter a port took shape in Mediterranean trading cities in the late Middle Ages, in response to repeated outbreaks of plague.",
    "Those who devised it had no idea that the disease was carried by microbes; many of them believed it spread through corrupted air.",
    "A waiting period long enough for the sick to show their illness could catch infections whatever their mechanism.",
    "The episode is sometimes told as a story of luck, as if officials stumbled on a sound practice for the wrong reasons.",
    "That reading undervalues what they did, which was to act on a pattern they could observe instead of waiting for a theory they did not have.",
    "Public health has often had to work this way, and it still does whenever a new illness spreads faster than its explanation."],
   "Yet the rule could still do some good, because it did not depend on knowing the cause, only on noticing that sickness tended to arrive with people and goods from places already afflicted.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "'Yet' sets the usefulness of the rule against the officials' ignorance of microbes, so the sentence must follow that ignorance. The next sentence, about a waiting period that works 'whatever their mechanism', spells out why not knowing the cause did not matter. The sentence therefore belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the ignorance of the cause has not yet been stated, so 'Yet' has nothing to oppose, and the claim that the rule did not depend on knowing the cause would precede any mention of the cause.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'That reading' from the story of luck it refers to.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already defended the officials and is generalising to public health; a sentence explaining why the rule worked belongs with the description of the rule."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as if it answered the story of luck, which it does only indirectly.",
   "V04A-quarantine-before-germs", "Gap (3) is tempting because the sentence defends the rule; its 'Yet' and the following 'whatever their mechanism' tie it to the ignorance of microbes.")

