sp("V04-A-012", "Science and environment", "Deltas shrink when dams starve them of sediment, so their protection lies upstream more than at the coast.", "Easy",
   ["A river delta is built from mud and sand that the river carries down from its basin and drops where it meets the sea.",
    "As long as this supply continues, the delta can keep pace with the slow sinking of its own soft ground and with the waves that wear its edges away.",
    "Large dams interrupt the process at its source.",
    "Their reservoirs trap much of the sediment that once travelled downstream, and the water released below them is comparatively clear.",
    "Deprived of fresh material, many deltas have begun to shrink, even where the sea itself has risen only a little.",
    "Coastal engineers can build walls against the waves, but no wall can replace the sediment that the river no longer brings."],
   "The protection of a delta, then, may depend less on such walls than on what happens hundreds of kilometres upstream.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence is a conclusion ('then') that refers back to 'such walls'. Walls are mentioned only in the last sentence, so the conclusion must come after it, at gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) no walls have been mentioned, and dams, the upstream cause, have not yet been introduced, so the conclusion has no basis.",
    3: "At gap (2) the sentence would separate 'Large dams interrupt the process' from the explanation of how their reservoirs trap sediment, and 'such walls' would have no referent.",
    5: "At gap (3) the walls have still not been mentioned, so 'such walls' has nothing to refer to."},
   "Placing the conclusion at gap (3), after the shrinking of deltas, without noticing that 'such walls' needs the sentence about coastal engineers.",
   "V04A-delta-sediment", "A conclusion locked by the backward reference 'such walls'.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

