sp("V04-A-014", "Psychology", "We value what we make because completing it is evidence of competence, not because of effort alone.", "Medium",
   ["People tend to value things more highly when they have helped to make them, even when the result is clumsy.",
    "A shelf assembled from a flat pack, a cake baked from a mix, a vegetable bed dug by hand: each seems worth more to its maker than an identical one bought ready-made.",
    "What seems to matter is completion, the sense of having turned raw materials into something that works.",
    "This suggests that the extra value is a kind of reward for competence, since the finished object becomes evidence of what its maker can do.",
    "Sellers have learned to use the tendency by leaving a small, satisfying step for the customer to complete.",
    "The step must be easy enough to succeed at, however, since a task that ends in failure produces frustration rather than pride."],
   "Yet effort alone seems not to be the cause, for a task that is abandoned halfway rarely leaves its maker with the same attachment.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence rules out effort as the cause, and the next sentence supplies the alternative: 'What seems to matter is completion'. The sentence must follow the examples of things people have made and precede the claim about completion, at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the sentence would come between the claim and the examples that illustrate it; the examples must follow the claim directly.",
    3: "At gap (3) completion has already been named as what matters, so denying that effort is the cause comes after the answer, and 'This suggests' would then point to the abandoned task instead of to completion.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph is discussing how sellers use the tendency; the question of what causes the tendency has been settled two sentences earlier."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as though it supported the point about completion after the fact.",
   "V04A-valuing-what-we-make", "A contrast marker with a forward link; gap (3) looks possible because the abandoned task also concerns completion.")

