sp("V04-A-015", "Society and culture", "Fashions return in cycles because each young generation rejects its parents' styles and revives older ones, in altered form.", "Easy",
   ["Styles of dress seem to return at fairly regular intervals: wide trousers, bold patterns and certain colours disappear for a couple of decades and then come back as if they were new.",
    "Part of the answer lies with the young, for whom a style their parents wore looks dated while a style their grandparents wore can look charmingly unfamiliar.",
    "Each generation of young buyers therefore rejects what it grew up seeing and reaches back past it to something its members never saw worn in earnest.",
    "Designers and shops, who need a reason to sell new clothes every season, are happy to encourage the revival.",
    "The returning style is rarely an exact copy, however; it comes back altered to suit new fabrics, new bodies and new ideas of comfort."],
   "Why should fashions move in such cycles rather than wandering at random?",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence is a question, and the next sentence begins 'Part of the answer', which needs a question before it. The question must follow the description of returning styles, so it belongs at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) part of the answer has already been given, so asking the question now comes after its answer, and 'Part of the answer' would have had nothing to answer.",
    3: "At gap (3) the sentence would interrupt the explanation, coming between the reasoning of the young and the role of the shops.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already explained the cycle and qualified it; the question that opens the explanation cannot come at the end."},
   "Placing the question at gap (2), as though the remark about the young were part of the description.",
   "V04A-fashion-cycles", "A forward set-up ('Part of the answer') makes the position plain.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 016
sp("V04-A-016", "History and politics", "Railways replaced local solar time with a standard time, making the timetable rather than the sun the measure of the hour.", "Medium",
   ["Until the nineteenth century, most towns kept their own time, set by the position of the sun, so that noon in one town fell a few minutes earlier or later than noon in the next.",
    "For travellers on foot or on horseback the differences hardly mattered, since no one moved fast enough to notice them.",
    "Railways changed this.",
    "A timetable that listed departures in a dozen local times was a recipe for missed connections and, on single tracks, for collisions.",
    "Railway companies therefore began to run their trains on a single standard time, and the towns along their lines gradually adjusted their clocks to match.",
    "Some resisted for a while, fitting the town clock with two minute hands, one for local time and one for the railway's."],
   "In the end, it was the timetable, not the sun, that told most people what time it was.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "'In the end' closes a story, and the story includes the resistance of towns that kept two minute hands. The conclusion must therefore follow the last sentence, at gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the railways have not yet arrived in the paragraph, so the victory of the timetable has no basis.",
    3: "At gap (2) the sentence would separate 'Railways changed this' from the explanation of why timetables needed one time.",
    5: "At gap (3) the conclusion would come before the resistance; 'In the end' must close the whole story, including the towns that held out, not interrupt it."},
   "Placing the conclusion at gap (3), right after towns adjusted their clocks, which looks like the end of the change.",
   "V04A-railway-time", "Gap (3) looks complete because the adoption has just been described; 'In the end' implies the resistance came first.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 017
sp("V04-A-017", "Economics and business", "Unpaid open-source work is partly explained by reputation, a reward that only open work can create.", "Medium",
   ["Software that anyone may copy, change and share for free poses an obvious puzzle for economics.",
    "Thousands of skilled programmers contribute to such projects without being paid by them, and the results compete with products built by firms that charge for theirs.",
    "Part of the answer is that many contributors are in fact paid, by employers who depend on the software and find it cheaper to improve a shared tool than to build a private one.",
    "For her, the rewards are of a different kind: the pleasure of solving a problem, the respect of peers who can see exactly what she did, and a public record of skill that later employers may value.",
    "The last of these rewards is the most economic, and it depends on openness itself, since a contribution hidden inside a firm's private code would be visible to no one outside it.",
    "The puzzle is partly dissolved, then, by noticing that open work produces a currency of reputation that closed work cannot."],
   "But this explains only the contributions that some company wants; it does not explain the volunteer who spends her evenings fixing an obscure fault.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence limits the employer explanation ('this explains only') and introduces 'the volunteer who spends her evenings'. The next sentence begins 'For her', which needs that volunteer. The sentence therefore belongs between the employer explanation and 'For her', at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the puzzle has been named but not described, and no explanation has been offered for 'this explains' to refer to.",
    2: "At gap (2) the employer explanation has not yet been given, so the sentence would limit an explanation the paragraph has not offered.",
    5: "At gap (4) the volunteer would appear only after 'For her' and after her rewards have been listed, leaving 'her' with no referent."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), reading 'this' as the puzzle itself.",
   "V04A-open-source-motives", "Two locks, 'this explains' and 'For her', but both need to be traced.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 018
sp("V04-A-018", "Science and environment", "Fungal networks do link plants, but calling them channels of cooperation between trees may misplace the purpose.", "Difficult",
   ["The roots of most land plants are joined to fungi that extend far into the soil, trading water and minerals for sugars that the plant makes in its leaves.",
    "Because a single fungus can connect to several plants at once, some writers have described forests as communities in which trees share food and warnings through an underground network.",
    "What remains uncertain is how much moves, how often, and whether the movement benefits the receiving plant rather than the fungus that carries it.",
    "A fungus is not a public utility; it is an organism with interests of its own, and it may move sugar wherever it gains most from doing so.",
    "Describing it as a channel for cooperation between trees may therefore attribute to the trees a purpose that belongs, if to anyone, to the fungus.",
    "None of this makes the network less remarkable; it makes the question of who is helping whom more interesting than the popular story allows."],
   "The picture is appealing, and it is not simply invented: substances have been traced moving from one plant to another through fungal threads.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "'The picture' refers to the description of forests as sharing communities, and the sentence concedes that something really does move between plants. The next sentence, 'What remains uncertain is how much moves', presupposes exactly that concession: one can ask how much moves only after it has been granted that something moves. The sentence therefore belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the forest-as-community picture has not been described, so 'The picture' has no referent; the sentence would also interrupt the explanation of what a single fungus connects.",
    3: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already turned to what is uncertain; restating the evidence for movement after raising doubts about its amount reverses the order of concession and doubt, and leaves 'how much moves' without its premise.",
    5: "At gap (4) 'The picture' would refer to the idea that the purpose belongs to the fungus, and a concession that the popular story has some basis would interrupt the closing sentence, which already balances the critique."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (4), as a concession just before the closing balance, because 'None of this makes the network less remarkable' also sounds conciliatory.",
   "V04A-fungal-networks", "Gap (4) offers a plausible concession point; only the presupposition in 'how much moves' shows that the concession must come before the doubts.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 019
sp("V04-A-019", "Philosophy and ethics", "Disputes about taste are real, settled by directing attention rather than by proof.", "Easy",
   ["It is often said that there is no arguing about taste.",
    "In such conversations, people rarely try to prove that a work is good; instead they point to features of it that the other person may have missed.",
    "Look at how the ending answers the opening, one says, or listen to what the bass is doing under the melody.",
    "The aim is not to win agreement by force of logic but to change what the other person perceives.",
    "When it works, the listener does not feel defeated; she feels that she now hears something she did not hear before.",
    "Disputes about taste are therefore real disputes, even if they are settled by attention rather than by proof."],
   "If that were true, however, the long conversations people have about films, novels and music would be pointless, and they plainly are not.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "'If that were true' refers to the saying that there is no arguing about taste, and the sentence introduces the 'long conversations' that the next sentence calls 'such conversations'. It must therefore come right after the opening sentence, at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) 'such conversations' in the preceding sentence would have had no referent, and 'that' would point to the claim that people point out features, not to the saying about taste.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate the aim of changing perception from the description of when it works.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already concluded that disputes about taste are real; objecting to the saying after this conclusion reverses the argument."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (4) because it seems to support the conclusion.",
   "V04A-arguing-about-taste", "A backward reference and a forward reference both point to the same gap.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 020
sp("V04-A-020", "Psychology", "Placebos change how illness is felt rather than the disease itself, so neither dismissal nor enthusiasm is right.", "Difficult",
   ["The placebo effect is often mentioned with a shrug, as if to say that a patient who improves after a sugar pill was never really ill, or only imagined getting better.",
    "Studies that separate different kinds of outcome suggest that the shrug is wrong in one way and right in another.",
    "On objective measures, such as the size of a tumour or the level of an infection, dummy treatments tend to achieve little.",
    "On symptoms that pass through experience, above all pain, nausea and fatigue, they can produce changes that patients report as genuine relief.",
    "It would be equally mistaken, however, to treat a dummy pill as a remedy like any other, since relief that rests on expectation may fade once the expectation is withdrawn."],
   "Those who shrug at the effect are therefore right about the tumour and wrong about the pain, and their error is to treat a change in how illness feels as no change at all.",
   [2, 3, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence draws a verdict ('therefore') from both kinds of outcome, so it must follow the sentences on tumours and on pain. It also names an error, and the last sentence, 'It would be equally mistaken, however', needs a mistake just named to be 'equally' mistaken with. The sentence therefore sits between the evidence on pain and the counter-warning, at gap (3).",
   {2: "At gap (1) neither kind of outcome has been described, so 'the tumour' and 'the pain' have no referents and 'therefore' has nothing to draw on.",
    3: "At gap (2) only the tumour has been discussed; 'the pain' has not yet appeared, and the verdict would split the paired evidence that it sums up.",
    5: "At gap (4) the verdict would follow the warning against overrating placebos, so 'equally mistaken' would have only the opening shrug to pair with, three sentences back, and the paragraph would end by returning to a point it had already balanced."},
   "Placing the verdict at gap (4), because a 'therefore' sentence looks like a natural ending.",
   "V04A-placebo-outcomes", "The end position is attractive for a 'therefore' verdict; only the word 'equally' in the last sentence shows that the verdict must precede it.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 021
sp("V04-A-021", "Society and culture", "The rule of silence between movements is a later convention with a real rationale and a real cost.", "Medium",
   ["Audiences at concerts of classical music are expected to stay silent between the movements of a symphony and to applaud only at the very end.",
    "Newcomers who clap after the first movement are met with frowns, and they may feel they have exposed their ignorance.",
    "Yet the rule is younger than it looks.",
    "The silence now demanded grew up later, alongside a view of the symphony as a single unbroken argument that applause would interrupt.",
    "There is something to be said for that view, since a pause between movements can itself be part of the music's effect.",
    "But a convention that makes first-time listeners feel unwelcome has a cost too, especially as many of the works were written for noisier halls."],
   "In earlier centuries, audiences often applauded between movements, and sometimes in the middle of one when a passage pleased them.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence is the evidence for the claim 'Yet the rule is younger than it looks', and the next sentence's 'grew up later' contrasts with its 'earlier centuries'. It must therefore come directly after that claim, at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the sentence would interrupt the description of the present rule before the newcomers' experience is told, and no claim about the rule's age has yet been made for it to support.",
    2: "At gap (2) the evidence would come before the claim it supports, and 'Yet the rule is younger than it looks' would then follow a description of earlier applause, so 'Yet' would set up a contrast with a sentence that already agrees with it.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has moved on to weighing the rule; evidence about its age belongs next to the claim about its age, three sentences earlier."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), before 'Yet the rule is younger than it looks', on the view that evidence can lead into its claim.",
   "V04A-concert-applause", "Gap (2) is tempting because evidence can precede a claim; the contrast between 'earlier centuries' and 'grew up later' fixes the order, and 'Yet' must turn against the present rule, not against the older practice.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 022
sp("V04-A-022", "History and politics", "Surnames began as descriptions added to tell people apart and only gradually became fixed hereditary labels.", "Easy",
   ["For much of history, most people in many parts of Europe had a single given name, and a village rarely needed more to tell its members apart.",
    "As populations grew and rulers began to tax and count their subjects more carefully, a single name was no longer enough.",
    "Many of these additions are still easy to read: a family called Smith or Baker descends, at some remove, from a person known by his work.",
    "At first such names could change from one generation to the next, since a baker's son might become a carter.",
    "Only gradually did they become fixed and hereditary, passed down whatever the bearer actually did for a living.",
    "The surname, in other words, began as a description and ended as a label."],
   "Clerks and neighbours added a second name to distinguish one John from another, drawn from a trade, a place, a father or some personal feature.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence describes the 'second name' that met the need stated just before it, and the following sentence refers to 'these additions'. It must therefore sit between the statement that a single name was no longer enough and 'Many of these additions', at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no need for a second name has yet arisen, so adding one has no motive.",
    3: "At gap (3) 'these additions' in the previous sentence would have had no referent, and the origin of the names would come after an example of them.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already traced the names to their fixed, hereditary form; describing how they were first added comes far too late."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3) because the example of Smith and Baker seems to introduce the topic of added names.",
   "V04A-surname-origins", "A backward reference ('these additions') makes the position plain.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 023
sp("V04-A-023", "Economics and business", "Insurance weakens care, so insurers leave part of the risk with the insured, which is what keeps cover affordable.", "Medium",
   ["Insurance exists to spread the cost of misfortune, but it can also change the behaviour of the people it protects.",
    "Economists call changes of this kind moral hazard, though the term implies more wickedness than the problem involves: neither the driver nor the firm has decided to cheat anyone.",
    "Insurers respond in ways that are familiar to anyone who has read a policy.",
    "They require the insured to bear the first part of any loss, offer lower premiums to those who install alarms, and refuse to pay where carelessness was gross.",
    "Each of these devices leaves the insured with some of the risk, which is precisely the point.",
    "Full protection would remove the very incentive that keeps losses rare, so a policy that covered everything would soon cost more than anyone would pay."],
   "A driver whose car is fully insured has a little less reason to park it carefully, and a firm insured against fire has a little less reason to spend money on sprinklers.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence gives examples of the change in behaviour claimed in the opening sentence, and it introduces the driver and the firm that the next sentence refers to with 'neither the driver nor the firm'. The examples therefore belong between the claim and the naming of moral hazard, at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the preceding sentence would already have spoken of 'the driver' and 'the firm' before either had been introduced, and the examples would interrupt the move from the problem to the insurers' response.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate the list of devices from 'Each of these devices', which refers to that list.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has concluded about full protection; illustrating the basic problem at the end comes too late."},
   "Placing the examples at gap (2), after the term is introduced, as if they illustrated the term.",
   "V04A-insurance-incentives", "Gap (2) is plausible because examples can follow a term; only the definite 'the driver' and 'the firm' in the naming sentence show that the examples must come first.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 024
sp("V04-A-024", "Science and environment", "The dark night sky shows that the universe is not unchanging, a conclusion reinforced by the expansion of space.", "Difficult",
   ["Why is the sky dark at night?",
    "The question sounds childish, but it troubled astronomers for centuries, because a simple argument seemed to show that the night sky should blaze.",
    "If the universe were infinite, unchanging and evenly filled with stars, then every line of sight, extended far enough, would end on the surface of some star.",
    "Distant stars appear fainter, but there would be more of them in proportion, and the two effects would cancel exactly.",
    "The whole sky would then shine about as brightly as the surface of a typical star.",
    "The darkness we see therefore tells us that at least one of the assumptions is false.",
    "The modern answer rejects the assumption that the universe is unchanging: it has a finite age, so light from stars beyond a certain distance has not yet had time to reach us."],
   "The expansion of space adds a further effect, stretching the light of distant sources until much of it falls outside the range our eyes can see.",
   [3, 4, 6, 7],
   "The missing sentence adds 'a further effect' that darkens the sky, and the expansion of space is a change in the universe. It therefore belongs to the modern answer, which rejects the assumption that the universe is unchanging, and must follow that answer at gap (4).",
   {3: "At gap (1) the sentence would interrupt the argument for a blazing sky between its premise and its account of faint stars; the expansion of space has no place inside an argument that assumes an unchanging universe.",
    4: "At gap (2) 'a further effect' seems to continue 'the two effects', but those effects cancel within the hypothetical unchanging universe; an expanding universe contradicts that hypothesis, and the sentence would break the step from cancelling effects to a blazing sky.",
    6: "At gap (3) the paragraph has just concluded that some assumption is false without saying which; a 'further' darkening effect would come before the first one, the finite age, has been given."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), reading 'a further effect' as a third item after the two effects that cancel.",
   "V04A-dark-night-sky", "Gap (2) shares the word 'effects'; rejecting it requires seeing that expansion contradicts the hypothesis being tested there.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 025
sp("V04-A-025", "Society and culture", "Museum labels shape looking, and the problem lies in reading before seeing, which some curators address by moving the text.", "Medium",
   ["A label beside an object in a museum seems a modest thing: a name, a date, a sentence or two of context.",
    "Yet it shapes looking more than most visitors realise, telling them what kind of attention to pay: whether to admire a technique, to place an object in history or to feel the weight of an event.",
    "None of this is sinister, since without labels most objects would remain mute to all but specialists.",
    "The difficulty lies in the order of events.",
    "Many visitors read the label first and then glance at the object to confirm what they have been told, so that they can leave having learned the label's lesson while hardly seeing what it described."],
   "Some curators have answered this by placing the text a few steps away, so that the eye meets the object before it meets the words.",
   [1, 3, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence answers a problem of order: it makes the eye meet 'the object before it meets the words'. The problem is that visitors read 'the label first', which the last sentence describes. The remedy must follow that description, at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the label has only been described as modest; no problem has been raised for curators to answer.",
    3: "At gap (2) 'this' would point to the claim that labels are not sinister, which is not a problem to be answered.",
    4: "At gap (3) the difficulty has been named but not explained; the remedy would come before the reader learns that visitors read the label first, and the explanation would then trail after its own solution."},
   "Placing the remedy at gap (3), straight after 'The difficulty lies in the order of events', because 'this' seems to refer to the difficulty.",
   "V04A-museum-labels", "Gap (3) is tempting because the difficulty has just been named; the remedy needs the explanation that follows it.")
